WEBVTT

00:00.040 --> 00:00.360
Okay.

00:00.360 --> 00:03.440
So now we're going to zoom in and focus on the component ETL.

00:03.480 --> 00:08.760
If you are building such a project, you're going to spend almost 90% just building this component,

00:08.800 --> 00:09.520
the ETL.

00:09.560 --> 00:12.040
So it is the core element of the data warehouse.

00:12.240 --> 00:16.640
And I want you to have a clear understanding what is exactly an ETL.

00:16.680 --> 00:19.280
So our data exists in a source system.

00:19.280 --> 00:24.480
And now what we want to do is, is to get our data from the source and move it to the target.

00:24.480 --> 00:26.720
Source and target could be like database tables.

00:26.840 --> 00:32.240
So now the first step that we have to do is to specify which data we have to load from the source.

00:32.280 --> 00:37.200
Of course we can say that we want to load everything, but let's say that we are doing incremental loads.

00:37.200 --> 00:42.520
So we're going to go and specify a subset of the data from the source in order to prepare it and load

00:42.520 --> 00:43.720
it later to the target.

00:43.720 --> 00:46.600
So this step in the ETL process we call it extracts.

00:46.600 --> 00:49.240
We are just identifying the data that we need.

00:49.280 --> 00:51.920
We pull it out and we don't change anything.

00:51.920 --> 00:54.720
It's going to be like 1 to 1 like the source system.

00:54.720 --> 01:00.760
So the extract has only one task to identify the data that you have to pull out from the source and

01:00.760 --> 01:01.880
to not change anything.

01:01.880 --> 01:04.080
So we will not manipulate the data at all.

01:04.240 --> 01:05.720
It can stay as it is.

01:05.720 --> 01:09.280
So this is the first step in the ETL process that extracts.

01:09.320 --> 01:13.280
Now moving on to the stage number two we're going to take this extracted data.

01:13.280 --> 01:16.360
And we will do some manipulations transformations.

01:16.640 --> 01:19.520
And we're going to change the shape of those data.

01:19.520 --> 01:22.040
And this process is really heavy working.

01:22.040 --> 01:28.440
We can do a lot of stuff like data cleansing, data integration and a lot of formatting and data normalization.

01:28.440 --> 01:30.480
So a lot of stuff we can do in this step.

01:30.480 --> 01:34.040
So this is the second step in the ETL process, the transformation.

01:34.040 --> 01:40.280
We're going to take the original data and reshape it, transform it into exactly the format that we

01:40.280 --> 01:44.680
need into a new format and shapes that we need for analysis and reporting.

01:44.720 --> 01:47.400
Now finally we get to the last step in the ETL process.

01:47.400 --> 01:48.600
We have the load.

01:48.600 --> 01:53.880
So in this step we're going to take this new data and we're going to insert it into the target.

01:53.880 --> 01:54.840
So it is very simple.

01:54.840 --> 01:58.240
We're going to take this prepared data from the transformation step.

01:58.240 --> 02:03.400
And we're going to move it into its final destination the target like for example data warehouse.

02:03.400 --> 02:05.280
So that's ETL in a nutshell.

02:05.320 --> 02:11.760
First extract the raw data, then transform it into something meaningful and finally load it to a target

02:11.760 --> 02:13.120
where it's going to make a difference.

02:13.120 --> 02:14.000
So that's it.

02:14.000 --> 02:16.840
This is what we mean with the ETL process.

02:16.840 --> 02:22.000
Now in real projects we don't have like only source and targets our data architecture can have like

02:22.000 --> 02:28.120
multiple layers depend on your design, whether you are building a warehouse or data lake or data warehouse.

02:28.120 --> 02:32.400
And usually there are like different ways on how to load the data between all those layers.

02:32.400 --> 02:35.930
And in order now to load the data from one layer to another one.

02:35.930 --> 02:39.170
There are like multiple ways on how to use the ETL process.

02:39.170 --> 02:44.330
So usually if you are loading the data from the source to the layer one like only extract the data from

02:44.330 --> 02:49.410
the source and load it directly to the layer number one without doing any transformations, because

02:49.410 --> 02:54.810
I want to see the data as it is in the first layer, and now between the layer number one and the layer

02:54.810 --> 03:01.130
number two, you might go and use the full ETL so we can extract from the layer one, transform it and

03:01.130 --> 03:03.170
then load it to the layer number two.

03:03.210 --> 03:05.810
So with that we are using the whole process the ETL.

03:05.850 --> 03:10.370
And now between layer two and layer three we can do only transformation and then load.

03:10.370 --> 03:15.730
So we don't have to deal with how to extract the data because it is maybe using the same technology.

03:15.730 --> 03:19.010
And we are taking all data from layer two to layer three.

03:19.010 --> 03:22.650
So we transform the whole layer two and then load it to layer three.

03:22.690 --> 03:26.130
And now between 3 and 4 you can use only the Elm.

03:26.130 --> 03:29.610
So maybe it's something like duplicating and replicating the data.

03:29.610 --> 03:31.890
And then you are doing the transformation.

03:31.890 --> 03:34.570
So you load to the new layer and then transform it.

03:34.610 --> 03:36.290
Of course this is not a real scenario.

03:36.290 --> 03:41.810
I'm just showing you that in order to move from source to a target, you don't have to always, to use

03:41.810 --> 03:45.330
a complete ETL, depend on the design of your data architecture.

03:45.330 --> 03:48.610
You might use only few components from the ETL.

03:48.650 --> 03:52.290
Okay, so this is how ETL looks like in real projects.
